  "text": {
   "approach": "Approach is up a brush-choked valley low in the Goodell Creek drainage — rough enough that even seasoned Cascades climbers have balked at it. This is the opposite (northeast) side of the peak from the standard Triumph Pass/Despair Lakes approach.",
   "approach_logistics": null,
   "road": null,
   "overview": "A ~3,700 ft previously-unclimbed buttress line up Mt. Despair's northeast side to its north summit, opened in 2014: an easy 5.6 opening corner, a series of notches requiring short rappels, a stout 5.9 pitch through a deep summit-area cleft, a further steep pitch to the north ridge, and a snow-ridge finish.",
   "description": null,
   "descent": "From the north summit, the first-ascent party exited over Triumph Pass and out the Thornton Lakes Trail rather than retracing the Goodell Creek approach."
  },
